    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A settlement called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Chengzhou</a> was built in 1036 BCE for captured Shang nobles, and the Eastern Zhou later made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>, 15 km to its west, and then Chengzhou itself their capital.</li><li>Luoyang was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> from 25 CE and was burned, ransacked or sacked in 189, 190, 311 and 534, each time recovering or being rebuilt, as under the Wei in 220 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in 604.</li><li>Under the Northern Wei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> it was successively a capital, the Eastern Capital and the Western Capital, with populations of 600,000 under the Jin and the Northern Wei and around one million under the Tang.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 166 CE the first Roman mission, sent by the king of Da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Andun, reached Luoyang after arriving by sea in what later became central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Andun is identified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> Antoninus, who reigned from 161 to 180.</p></aside>
    </section>

